{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Maintainer Architecture Overview",
  "description": "Core architecture walkthrough for developers maintaining ai-info-collector: from configuration through RSS collection, LLM-based analysis, to markdown report generation.",
  "ref": "main",
  "isPrimary": true,
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "README.md",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Project Overview",
      "description": "**Situation:** You're looking at ai-info-collector, a Python CLI tool that synthesizes AI news at scale.\n\n**Mechanism:** The application implements a 3-stage pipeline: (1) fetch articles from RSS/Atom feeds configured in TOML, (2) deduplicate and filter by freshness, (3) analyze each article with a local GGUF model to summarize and assign literacy levels, (4) generate markdown reports bucketed by literacy level.\n\n**Implication:** As a maintainer, you need to understand the data contracts between stages—articles flow through `Article` domain objects, analysis results are `AnalysisResult` objects, and configuration is Pydantic-validated. Changes in one stage's output directly impact downstream stages.\n\n**Gotcha:** The app is **local-only** by design (no cloud LLM). The model path resolution is relative to the project root, which happens in `domain.py`'s `model_post_init()`. If tests or CI runs from different directories, model loading will fail silently unless you understand this."
    },
    {
      "directory": "src/ai_info_collector",
      "title": "Module Layout",
      "description": "**Situation:** The `src/ai_info_collector/` directory contains 13 Python modules, each with a focused responsibility.\n\n**Mechanism:** The codebase follows a clean architecture pattern:\n- **Domain layer:** `domain.py` defines all Pydantic models (`SourceConfig`, `AppConfig`, `Article`, `AnalysisResult`, `FilterConfig`).\n- **Orchestration:** `pipeline.py` owns the main processing loop.\n- **Subsystems:** `collection.py` (RSS fetching), `analysis.py` (LLM inference), `report.py` (markdown output).\n- **Plumbing:** `settings.py` (config loading), `feed_discovery.py` (auto-detect RSS URLs), `http_client.py` (retry logic), `url_utils.py` (URL normalization).\n- **CLI:** `cli.py` wires typer commands to subsystems.\n\n**Implication:** When adding a feature (new analysis method, new report format, new config option), you'll likely touch: domain models first → subsystem logic → CLI command → tests. This flow is rigid for good reason.\n\n**Gotcha:** `analysis.py` and `collection.py` define Protocol interfaces (`ArticleAnalyzer`, `ArticleCollector`), not concrete classes. This makes testing easy but can be confusing on first read—the real implementations are `LlamaCppAnalyzer` and `RssCollector`."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/domain.py",
      "line": 6,
      "selection": [6, 32],
      "title": "Domain Models: Configuration",
      "description": "**Situation:** This section defines the four root configuration objects: `SourceConfig` (one RSS feed), `CollectionConfig` (feed list + retry policy), `AnalysisConfig` (LLM settings), `OutputConfig` (where to write reports).\n\n**Mechanism:** All use Pydantic v2 `BaseModel` with field constraints (min_length, ge, le). The `AnalysisConfig.model_post_init()` hook (not shown here) resolves relative model paths to absolute paths using the repo root as the anchor.\n\n**Implication:** Configuration is **immutable after load**—the CLI loads it once, passes it through the pipeline, and each subsystem gets its slice (collection uses `CollectionConfig`, analyzer uses `AnalysisConfig`). This makes testing deterministic.\n\n**Gotcha:** `HttpUrl` fields require fully qualified URLs (https://...). If a test or config uses a bare URL, Pydantic will raise a validation error. The error message is clear, but it breaks early in `settings.py:load_config()`."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/domain.py",
      "line": 40,
      "selection": [40, 50],
      "title": "Domain Models: Literacy Levels",
      "description": "**Situation:** `DEFAULT_LITERACY_LEVEL_DEFINITIONS` maps 3 literacy levels to Japanese descriptions for AI learners—basic users, practitioners, and leaders.\n\n**Mechanism:** These strings are embedded in the prompt sent to the LLM. The analyzer classifies each article into one of these 3 levels, and reports are bucketed accordingly.\n\n**Implication:** If you change literacy level semantics (add a level 4, rename level 2), you must:\n1. Update this dict\n2. Update the prompt in `analysis.py:_build_prompt()`\n3. Update tests in `test_analysis.py` to expect the new level\n4. Coordinate with `report.py` to ensure output files match (e.g., `level_4.md`)\n\n**Gotcha:** The LLM is told to return JSON with a `literacy_level` field. If the prompt and this dict go out of sync, the LLM might return level 4 while the report generator expects levels 1–3 only—leading to silent errors in report filtering."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/cli.py",
      "line": 17,
      "selection": [17, 50],
      "title": "CLI: Entry Point and Logging Setup",
      "description": "**Situation:** The CLI entry point uses `typer` to expose two commands: `run` (main pipeline) and `sources` (feed discovery).\n\n**Mechanism:** `_create_analyzer()` instantiates the LLM backend. `_configure_logging()` sets up a `TimedRotatingFileHandler` that rotates logs at midnight, retaining the last `retention_days` files (default 3). The root logger is cleared and reconfigured each time to ensure clean state.\n\n**Implication:** Logging configuration is **stateful**—if you run the CLI twice in the same Python process (unlikely but possible in tests), the second run clears handlers from the first. This is intentional but worth noting.\n\n**Gotcha:** `TimedRotatingFileHandler` with `backupCount=retention_days - 1` means if retention is 3 days, only 2 backup files are kept (today + 2 prior days = 3 total). The math is off-by-one. If you want to keep exactly 3 days, set `backupCount=retention_days - 1` (which it does), but understand this is a Python logging quirk, not a bug."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/collection.py",
      "line": 20,
      "selection": [20, 45],
      "title": "Article Collection: Deduplication & Freshness",
      "description": "**Situation:** `filter_fresh_articles()` and `deduplicate_articles()` are helper functions that clean the raw feed parse output before analysis.\n\n**Mechanism:** Fresh articles pass a date cutoff (now ± freshness_days). Deduplication tracks both URLs (normalized) and titles (lowercased + whitespace normalized) to catch mirrors and reposts. Duplicates are dropped silently.\n\n**Implication:** If an article URL is normalized differently than expected, it might slip through as a duplicate or fail to deduplicate. The `url_utils.normalize_url()` function is critical—check it if dedup behavior surprises you.\n\n**Gotcha:** The deduplication loop visits articles in feed order and stops on first match. If two feeds both carry the same article, only the first occurrence is kept. This is deterministic but depends on feed order in config. If you need last-writer-wins or smarter merge, this function is a hot spot."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/collection.py",
      "line": 45,
      "selection": [45, 70],
      "title": "RSS Collection: Main Loop",
      "description": "**Situation:** `RssCollector.collect()` is the main collection subsystem. It iterates over configured sources, fetches their feeds (with retry logic), parses them, and returns collected articles and errors.\n\n**Mechanism:** The HTTP client is injected in the constructor (dependency injection pattern), making unit tests easy. Each source fetch may fail—errors are accumulated and returned, but collection continues. Articles from failed sources are skipped, not faked.\n\n**Implication:** Error handling is **graceful degradation**—if one feed times out or 404s, the pipeline continues with the other feeds. The CLI logs errors but exits 0. If you're monitoring feed health, check the returned `errors` list, not just exit codes.\n\n**Gotcha:** `feedparser` is permissive—it will parse malformed RSS/Atom feeds and return partial data without raising exceptions. A feed might claim 10 articles but parse only 3. If you need strict validation, add it here."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/pipeline.py",
      "line": 16,
      "selection": [16, 40],
      "title": "Pipeline Orchestration",
      "description": "**Situation:** `CollectionPipeline.run()` is the heart of the application—it sequences collection, deduplication, filtering, per-article analysis, and reporting.\n\n**Mechanism:** The pipeline uses dependency injection: `collector` and `analyzer` are Protocol interfaces, allowing you to swap in fakes for testing. The loop enforces `max_items` per source—once a source hits its quota, further articles are dropped. Analysis results and collected items are paired and returned.\n\n**Implication:** The pipeline is stateless—it doesn't cache results or maintain side effects. Each run is independent. If you add caching (to avoid re-analyzing the same article), do it in the subsystem (e.g., `LlamaCppAnalyzer`), not in the pipeline.\n\n**Gotcha:** The `source_limits` dict is built once, before the loop. If `config.collection.sources` changes during iteration (which it shouldn't), the behavior is undefined. Sources are identified by name—if two sources share a name, the limit calculation breaks. The code assumes source names are unique (not validated), so this is a quiet landmine."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/analysis.py",
      "line": 42,
      "selection": [42, 62],
      "title": "LLM Analysis: Prompt & JSON Parsing",
      "description": "**Situation:** `LlamaCppAnalyzer.analyze()` sends a prompt to the local LLM and parses the JSON response to extract summary, category, literacy level, and reasoning.\n\n**Mechanism:** The backend uses `llama_cpp.Llama.create_chat_completion()` with `response_format={\"type\": \"json_object\"}` to grammar-constrain the model to emit valid JSON. The response is unwrapped from the OpenAI-like completion format and returned as a string.\n\n**Implication:** The prompt construction (in `_build_prompt()`, not shown) is crucial—it must unambiguously tell the LLM what JSON shape to return. If the prompt is vague, the LLM might return valid JSON but with unexpected field names or values, breaking the downstream parser.\n\n**Gotcha:** `llama_cpp` is a thin wrapper around llama.cpp's C++ code. Model loading is lazy—the first call to `analyze()` will load the GGUF file and initialize the model. This is slow. If you want to warm up the model, call `analyze()` with a dummy article early in `main()`, or refactor to eager-load in `__init__()`. Also, if `n_threads` doesn't match your CPU count, inference can be surprisingly slow."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/ai_info_collector/report.py",
      "line": 5,
      "selection": [5, 30],
      "title": "Report Generation: Markdown Rendering",
      "description": "**Situation:** `render_markdown()` and `write_markdown()` generate human-readable markdown for each article-result pair. The full `write_level_reports()` function (not shown) buckets results by literacy level and writes separate `.md` files.\n\n**Mechanism:** For each (article, analysis result) tuple, the code emits a structured markdown block with metadata (source, date, category, level) and the LLM's summary. All articles flow into a single list and are joined with newlines.\n\n**Implication:** The markdown format is fixed—if you want to change the template (e.g., add reasoning, reorder fields, use different headings), edit this function. The format is Japanese-focused (headings in Japanese, structure borrowed from technical reports).\n\n**Gotcha:** `render_markdown()` doesn't escape markdown special characters in titles or summaries. If an article title contains `#` or `[]`, the markdown output will be malformed. If this becomes an issue, pass summaries through a markdown escaper."
    },
    {
      "file": "tests/test_pipeline.py",
      "line": 1,
      "selection": [1, 45],
      "title": "Testing: Fake Implementations & Protocol Verification",
      "description": "**Situation:** The test file demonstrates how to stub the `ArticleCollector` and `ArticleAnalyzer` Protocols using `FakeCollector`, `FakeAnalyzer`, and more specialized fakes (`MultiArticleCollector`, `FilteringAnalyzer`).\n\n**Mechanism:** Each fake returns hardcoded `CollectionResult` or `AnalysisResult` objects, allowing tests to verify the pipeline's logic (filtering, deduplication, source limits) without touching the LLM or network. Tests import directly from submodules, not from `__init__.py`.\n\n**Implication:** When adding a new feature to the pipeline (e.g., a new filtering rule), add a test case here with a tailored fake. The protocol pattern makes this easy and forces you to think about the contract, not implementation details.\n\n**Gotcha:** Fakes return hardcoded `datetime.now(timezone.utc)`. If your test depends on exact timestamp comparisons or runs across a date boundary, it might fail intermittently. Use a dependency-injected `now` function or freeze the datetime in tests (e.g., with `freezegun`)."
    },
    {
      "file": "pyproject.toml",
      "line": 1,
      "selection": [1, 30],
      "title": "Project Metadata & Dependency Management",
      "description": "**Situation:** `pyproject.toml` is the project manifest—it declares dependencies, test config, lint rules, and build system.\n\n**Mechanism:** Dependencies use version ranges (e.g., `httpx>=0.27,<1`) to allow patch updates but lock major versions. Optional `[dev]` dependencies include pytest and coverage. The project targets Python 3.11+. Ruff is configured for linting with specific rules (E, F, I = errors, undefined names, imports).\n\n**Implication:** If you add a new subsystem (e.g., database support, new report format), add its dependency here with version constraints. If CI fails on a lint error, check Ruff's rules and `.ruff.toml` (not shown, but check if it exists).\n\n**Gotcha:** `uv` is the package manager (mentioned in README as the preferred way to sync). `pip install -e .` will work but bypasses `uv.lock`, risking dependency version drift. Always use `uv sync` for local development and `uv pip compile` for CI builds to ensure reproducibility."
    },
    {
      "file": "config/default.toml",
      "line": 1,
      "title": "Configuration Example: Sources & Analysis Settings",
      "description": "**Situation:** `default.toml` is the user-facing configuration file. It defines RSS sources, analysis model settings, and output paths.\n\n**Mechanism:** The `[collection]` section lists sources with name, URL, optional feed URL, and max items per source. The `[analysis]` section sets LLM parameters (model path, context size, threads, temperature). The `[output]` section specifies where to write reports.\n\n**Implication:** When debugging user issues (\"reports are empty,\" \"model won't load\"), start by validating their config file against `domain.py`'s Pydantic models. Use `python -m ai_info_collector run --config <file>` to catch validation errors early.\n\n**Gotcha:** Model paths are relative to the project root. If a user runs the CLI from a different directory (e.g., `cd /tmp && python -m ai_info_collector ...`), the relative path resolution in `AnalysisConfig.model_post_init()` will fail. Document this clearly or use absolute paths in examples."
    },
    {
      "content": "## You now understand the maintainer's mental model\n\n**You can now:**\n- Add a new RSS source by editing the config\n- Modify the LLM analysis logic (prompt, JSON schema, model settings)\n- Add new literacy level definitions\n- Change report markdown format\n- Extend the CLI with new commands (e.g., `validate-config`, `test-feed`)\n- Debug issues by tracing the pipeline: collection → deduplication → filtering → analysis → reporting\n\n**Key dependencies:**\n- Domain models (`domain.py`) define the data contracts\n- Configuration flows from `settings.py` through the pipeline to subsystems\n- Logging is configured once in `cli.py`; subsystems use `logging.getLogger(__name__)`\n- All LLM inference happens in `LlamaCppAnalyzer`—swap the backend here for cloud API support (future work)\n\n**Follow-up tours:**\n- **Feature: Adding a New Analysis Method** — walk through adding a new classification dimension (e.g., sentiment, bias detection)\n- **Bug Investigation: Feed Parsing Failures** — how to trace and fix issues with specific RSS sources\n- **Optimization: Batch LLM Inference** — refactoring the per-article analysis loop for parallel or batch processing\n\n**Landing zone:** Check `CONTRIBUTING.md` for code style and PR process. Run `uv run pytest` locally before pushing. The test suite enforces 90% coverage (`--cov-fail-under=90`)."
    }
  ]
}
